cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Please cite this software version and the associated paper."
type: software
title: "Lean proofs for root-sparsity Hamiltonian simulation, dissipative differential equations and linear systems"
version: 0.1.0-rc30
date-released: 2026-11-07
authors:
  - family-names: Berry
    given-names: Dominic W.
  - family-names: Rawat
    given-names: Abhishek S.
url: "https://github.com/DominicWB/sparse-quantum-lean"
repository-code: "https://github.com/DominicWB/sparse-quantum-lean"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - Lean
  - quantum algorithms
  - Hamiltonian simulation
  - differential equations
  - linear systems
abstract: >-
  Formalized primitive sparse-oracle query bounds for time-independent and
  time-dependent Hamiltonian simulation, constant-coefficient and
  time-dependent dissipative linear differential equations, general
  constant-coefficient differential equations and linear-system solving,
  time-dependent versions on nonuniform meshes with integrated sample-error
  certificates and time-indexed block encodings, positive Chebyshev filtering
  for the linear-system algorithm, query lower bounds for Hamiltonian
  simulation and linear-system solving (also against adaptive algorithms and
  algorithms with measurements inside a run) and
  for dissipative differential equations, and gate and qubit counts for
  gate-efficient Hamiltonian simulation and dissipative differential
  equations, with exact gates, with native oracle implementations, for
  time-independent and time-dependent Hamiltonian simulation and
  constant-coefficient dissipative differential equations from binary sparse
  oracles, and over a finite gate set with single-qubit synthesis and the
  decomposition of controlled gates as hypotheses, also with supplied
  finite-set circuits retained and charged linearly, and with the manuscript's
  filter schedule, slot counts and repetition counts, together with the
  finite clock weights, the operator tail estimate, the simulation theorem
  for indefinite factors, the native sparse factor, the large-sparsity core and
  the combined upper bound of optimal-precision Hamiltonian simulation and of
  refined-precision unitary implementation, and two-norm Hamiltonian simulation
  with a supplied entry bound, together with their matching query lower bounds
  (also against adaptive algorithms and algorithms with measurements inside a
  run), and gate and qubit counts for optimal-precision and two-norm
  Hamiltonian simulation, with exact gates and with native private calls,
  including stable local signed factors and controlled native locators, and
  time-dependent dissipative differential equations with a general source
  interface, also on nonuniform meshes, with approximately implemented
  preparations, in the zero-time and zero-generator cases and with represented
  samples of any sign and with preparations on temporary work registers,
  gate-level dissipative differential equations with source circuits at the
  manuscript's precision, and general constant-coefficient differential equations
  from a mixed-output linear-system solver, gate-level Hamiltonian simulation
  from sparse access of general size with general decoders, gate-level dissipative
  ODE solvers from sparse access of general size with exact binary entry
  decoding, also under the manuscript's source and sample certificates,
  together with conformance
  statements of the headline results, with additional formal hypotheses and
  restrictions identified in the statement guides.
  The project targets Lean and Mathlib 4.32.1 and includes checked axiom audits.
